We examine new physics (NP) within the $b 	o u \ell  ar{\ell}$ transition, specifically focusing on the rare $b 	o u 	au  ar{	au}$ decay. While current experimental rates for this transition are still at early stages, theoretical insights allow for robust constraints on NP couplings present in the $b 	o u 	au  ar{	au}$ transition by leveraging the well-established absence of NP in the $b 	o c 	au  ar{
u}$ process. We utilize future projections of the LHCb experiment, specifically the LHCb-II upgrade, to perform this analysis. Our results indicate that LHC-II will achieve sensitivity sufficient to set limits on NP couplings in $b 	o u 	au  ar{
u}$ that are approximately an order of magnitude tighter than those derivable from the $b 	o s 	au  ar{
u}$ transition, given the significant coupling correlations between these sectors.
